# Post-mortem -- slice 0210, attempt 01

## Summary

Step 0210 is implemented and was judged review-green on cycle 2; build, elevation and constructor-unit-tests passed, but the supervisor killed ui-smoke at its 180 s deadline before the summary printed, and the arc escalated on "verification environment/deadline evidence".
The suite still passes (237 of 237), but it now needs about 192 s. Its frozen deadline was never raised as the suite grew from 25 s to 176 s across the arc, despite 27 near-budget warnings, and this step's eleven built-in item models pushed it over.

## Root cause

The single load-bearing cause is the **ui-smoke gate descriptor's deadline**. The ui-smoke entry in `specs/0050/.spec-gates` (lines 98–125) has no `attempt_timeout_seconds`. It only has `runtime_estimate_seconds: 180` and a stale `runtime_actual_whole_seconds: 27`. ASG's `deadlines.effective_attempt_timeout` (`multistep/_arc_runner/gates/deadlines.py:8–20`) falls back to `runtime_estimate_seconds` as the hard kill. `evaluate_gate.py:836` resolves `first_timeout` through it, and `evaluate_gate.py:1061–1069` returns `OUTCOME_TIMED_OUT` with "first attempt timed out after 180s". `run_phases.py:749` turns that result into the escalation reason.

The run did not fail any test. The worker's two development runs of the same command on the same tree took 193.7 s and 192.0 s, with 237 passed (`.artifacts/0210-ui-smoke-1.log`, `0210-ui-smoke-final.log`). The TRX wall clock was 12:30:45.9 → 12:33:56.8 Z. The supervisor's run was cut at 180.3 s (log line 16824).

The nearest green case under the same rule is ui-smoke at step 0202 (idx 201), which passed in 173.7 s (line 16078). The suite has been within 2–5 % of the deadline since step 0191: 175.3 s, then 171.4, 171.8, 176.2, 174.1, 172.0 and 173.7 s.

This step adds eleven entries to `Catalogue/Builtin/`, which every app start imports (AGENTS.md: `importOnStart` checks `Catalogue/Builtin/` on every start). That added the last ~18 s. The added time is sanctioned product growth, not a defect.

The long-term driver is also product growth the spec sanctions. Since step 0001 the case count rose only 14 % (208 → 237) while wall time rose ×7. The step-to-step jumps line up with wiring steps that put more start-up work into every headless host:

- 0088: the app runs on the library database;
- 0095: the catalogue import is wired over the database;
- 0112: start-up imports open the import dialog from the Launcher;
- 0120: the Library window gains export;
- 0154 and 0156: kits open, and opening one resolves its library snapshot;
- 0191: every window registers under its kit.

The TRX holds 3,184 s of test time across 237 parallel tests, so per-host start-up cost dominates.

Two tool-side factors let the deadline arrive as a kill instead of a decision:

- **Warnings only reached the log.** `budget_watch.budget_near` logged `GATE-BUDGET-NEAR` 27 times before this run, at up to 98 % of budget. `boot_budget_warnings` wrote "WARN gate budget: gate ui-smoke ran 159s of its 180s … suggest attempt_timeout_seconds: 240" at four boots (lines 11742, 11797, 11844, 14386). Neither reached the notification channel.
- **The worker could not see the deadline.** Neither the worker's task file nor the implement-worker and arc-runner system prompts mention a gate deadline (grep for timeout, deadline and runtime_estimate finds nothing). So the worker saw 3 m 13 s and reported ui-smoke "237 passed, unchanged" (SoW, Tests). The judge reviews before the gates run, so it could not see it either.

Neither factor is the owner: the descriptor's deadline is.

There is no change of provider or model since the last green slice, and this is the first deadline escalation in the arc, so this is not a repeat.

